<project@phoenix.net>, on 23 Apr 1995 16:51:16 GMT:

> Publishing the evidence is not a copyright violation from what I can
> tell.

I agree. Sections 107 and 108 of the copyright law protect
fair use of copyrighted work. Everyone is protected, from
librarians or professors photocopying copyrighted documents
to investigative journalists and historians quoting from
private letters, diaries, and memoranda.

Furthermore the law provides no financial or punitive relief
for infringement of copyrighted work not registered with the
Copyright Office. As any registered work is deposited into
the Library of Congress for public retrieval, one presumes
that the Church of Scientology documents have not been
registered.

At great legal expense, the owner of an unregistered work
may do no more than seek an injunction preventing or
restraining infringement. The plaintiff may recover only
actual damages, which are difficult to prove. No other
redress for infringement of copyrighted work may be gained
under present law.

I approve reforming copyright law to strengthen fair use and
to give unregistered work the same protection from
infringement enjoyed by published work.

The Church of Scientology has also cited posting of "trade
secrets." To my knowledge, the law does not protect secrets;
it protects only privacy. If you can learn my secrets
without invading my privacy -- without stealing or unlawful
eavesdropping -- or without violating a contract with me,
you may use my secrets as you please.

Private citizens may have limited exception to that sweeping
principle, but public figures, activists, and advocacy
groups, like the Church of Scientology, do not. Otherwise
there would be no such thing as investigative journalism,
whose main purpose is to expose secrets.
