>However, I'd hate to be the one taken to court on the
>alt.sex.pedophilia newsgroup.  I certainly can't afford to defend the
>suit even if I believed in the issue.

I understand the very difficult position that system administrators
at institutions like universities and freenets find themselves in.
If they *don't* censor, are they realistically risking criminal liability?
(if you think this is a realistic risk, perhaps you could tell me how many
of the *numerous* sites that carry alt.whatever-you-don't-like in Canada
has ever been charged)
If they *do* censor, aren't they deciding what a large community can or cannot
read or write?  In doing so, don't they risk infringing Charter Rights?

The comment about being able to "afford to defend the suit"
is probably realistic, but still somewhat unsettling.  Is Canada
a place where only the rich have freedom of expression?


>In my judgement, distributing these breaks the law.
>If I don't remove them, I'm in a position of feeling I'm breaking the law.

If it was your own personal decision, then do whatever you like -- it's a
free country.  The problem is that you're deciding for more than yourself;
you're deciding for some 30,000 other people (or however large your online
community has grown).

Is Bell Canada breaking the law when it allows a young child to
dial a 976-number?  I know they have "common carrier" protection
and computer communication systems don't (at least yet).

Would you consider it a solution if there was some legislation
that removed any liability for someone whose computer system
merely provides a "conduit" for someone else's information?
What about someone who merely provides PPP service that allows someone
else to download naughty stuff?  Are they liable?  Should they be?

I think it is important to decide whether you want to censor
alt.whatever because of your fear of legal liability, or is
it that you disagree with the content on a more personal level?


>Should I continue to do so, get charged and let the court decide?

I wouldn't ask you to take the heat for an issue that affects
everyone, but then who will?  Freedom of expression is an individual
right, and if no one will stand up for it, then it will wither and die.

Let me be clear on this.  I'll moan and groan if the Ottawa Freenet
censors newsgroups, but I'm not ready to say it's your "responsibility"
to carry all Usenet groups.

I worry though, that since Freenets get so much government funding,
don't we risk entering a climate where community networks end up
silencing speech that is merely controversial (and not illegal)
so as not to jeopardize the government moolah?


>Do these same principles apply in all other issues in my life
>in society: I can break the law knowingly then wait to the court
>to reaffirm my understanding when I'm caught?

Of course if you break the law you risk punishment, ... and rightly so.

Let's not lose sight of what is the real issue here.

Should you, for fear that *some* Usenet messages *might* be illegal,
censor a whole topic of discussion?  What about the other articles?
What is the status of the constitutionally protected speech that you
have silenced?

If you accept that the carrier of information is indeed liable for
transmitting someone else's expressions, then are you not at risk
of someone maliciously transmitting information through your
computer for the sole purpose of burdening you with legal problems?

What would you do if, as our neighbours to the south joked,
someone posted some "illegal messages" to "rec.hockey"?
Canadians simply would not stand for "rec.hockey" being dropped
from their Usenet newsfeed, right?  Would you then resort to forging
cancels of individual Usenet articles?  Do you have the time and energy
to scan articles in "rec.hockey"?  Should the government realistically
hold you accountable for its content?



Coming back to your initial comment, ...

I'm glad you've said we agree on principle.

When you want to delete a newsgroup from Carleton or the Ottawa Freenet,
who do you ask for permission?

Can you give us a list of Usenet newsgroups that you consider illegal
in Canada?  I'm sure you'd be doing the Canadian net community a service.
I'm not trying to be cute here.  There just isn't very much consensus
about what forms of expression are illegal and what aren't.  Canada's
