In <3njep3$oi6@insight.dcss.mcmaster.ca> djones@insight.mcmaster.ca (David Jones) writes:

>In article <daves.798775578@superior>,
>Dave Sutherland <daves@superior.carleton.ca> wrote:
>>In <3nh5ol$nns@insight.dcss.mcmaster.ca> djones@insight.mcmaster.ca (David Jones) writes:
>>

[...]

>Would you consider it a solution if there was some legislation
>that removed any liability for someone whose computer system
>merely provides a "conduit" for someone else's information?
>What about someone who merely provides PPP service that allows someone
>else to download naughty stuff?  Are they liable?  Should they be?

This is clearly what is required.  The IHAC has recommended that the
government study the need for this type of legislation.  This is both
good news and bad:  any legislative clarification will come under
pressure to put the burden of responsibility for content on the
carrier.  The ideal legislation will remove that responsibility.  In
its place, however, I'd be in favour of requiring operators to keep
proper records to allow the identification of any individual who broke
the law.  That way, the appropriate authorities could bring a charge
against whomever broke whatever law (the issue goes well beyond
"offensive content" and includes defamation, copyright violation,
fraud, computer security penetration etc.).  No new laws are required
for cyberspace but the existing laws should be enforced and enforcable.

>I think it is important to decide whether you want to censor
>alt.whatever because of your fear of legal liability, or is
>it that you disagree with the content on a more personal level?

A complex issue indeed.  Some would say "get rid of alt.skinheads
because people posting there say things that contravene the Human
Rights Act".  From my limited study of the newsgroup, that may be
true.  However, the debate is balanced and, in my opinion, the guys in
the white hats win hands down.  So, it's a healthy situation.

On the other hand, alt.pedophile is not a forum for debate.  It's a
distribution medium for material regarding an illegal activity: sex
with children.  In fact, the photographs of real children clearly
exploit the subject.  I have a real problem with this.


[...]

>I worry though, that since Freenets get so much government funding,
>don't we risk entering a climate where community networks end up
>silencing speech that is merely controversial (and not illegal)
>so as not to jeopardize the government moolah?

A very legitimate concern.  Our objective at the NCF is to obtain
funding from as wide a range of sources as possible to prevent this.
The protection is double:  we don't care if one source dries up for
ideological reasons and it's unlikely that the funder will withdraw
under pressure if it's clear his responsibility is limited.

For a similar reason, we are formalizing the relationship between the
NCF and the University.  We don't want Carleton to feel responsible
for what happens on the NCF and pull the plug.


[...]

>Should you, for fear that *some* Usenet messages *might* be illegal,
>censor a whole topic of discussion?  What about the other articles?
>What is the status of the constitutionally protected speech that you
>have silenced?

Always the quandry.  As in most things in life, there are difficult
tradeoffs.  While people have a right to freedom of speech, I'm not
sure that right guarantees that any medium of communications
system must allow them to express it.  Nor am I sure that that right
requires me, as a system operator, to provide the means for that
communication. 

In any case: what's illegal?  Is the definition of illegal: "what the
police will charge you for if you do"? Or "what a reasonable person
would judge to be against the law"?

>If you accept that the carrier of information is indeed liable for
>transmitting someone else's expressions, then are you not at risk
>of someone maliciously transmitting information through your
>computer for the sole purpose of burdening you with legal problems?

A good point.  Again calls for the need for legislation clarifying the
liability. (or we could allow a body of case law to develop).

>What would you do if, as our neighbours to the south joked,
>someone posted some "illegal messages" to "rec.hockey"?
>Canadians simply would not stand for "rec.hockey" being dropped
>from their Usenet newsfeed, right?  Would you then resort to forging
>cancels of individual Usenet articles?  Do you have the time and energy
>to scan articles in "rec.hockey"?  Should the government realistically
>hold you accountable for its content?

Presumably you might be held accountable for what your could
reasonably be expected to know about.  If you set up a bbs for the
express purpose of distributing licenced software, you should be
accountable.  If someone distributes it entirely without your
knowledge, you should not.  What should you do if it is brought to
your attention that specific copyrighted material is being distributed
via your system?  Would you not then be liable if you chose to do
nothing about it?



>Coming back to your initial comment, ...

>I'm glad you've said we agree on principle.

>When you want to delete a newsgroup from Carleton or the Ottawa Freenet,
>who do you ask for permission?

At Carleton, there's a committee.  The grounds seem to be application
of the law.  The NCF gets their newsfeed from Carleton but has
previously decided not to censor.

>Can you give us a list of Usenet newsgroups that you consider illegal
>in Canada?  I'm sure you'd be doing the Canadian net community a service.
>I'm not trying to be cute here.  There just isn't very much consensus
>about what forms of expression are illegal and what aren't.  Canada's
>laws limiting freedom of expression are notoriously difficult to interpret.
>I think the recent court decision about Eli Langer's "art" being given
>the court's seal of approval is a prime example.

True enough.  Nothing's easy in this life.  That doesn't mean you
shouldn't have a position on the matter.  I'll stick with
alt.pedophile as my test case for the moment.  However, I've also
wondered for some time about the morality of alt.binaries.pictures.*;
not at all because of erotic photographs but because there can be very
little doubt that the copyright holders for the scanned images almost
certainly haven't been consulted.  The issue doesn't come up at
Carleton or on the NCF any more since we ran out of disk space.



