*****************************************************************
NEWS RELEASE:  #95-221
*****************************************************************
TO:     Mailing List Members
FROM:   ANSWP News Service
        American National Socialist Workers' Party, Inc.
DATE:   26 April 95
RE:     Germany's current reaction to Mein Kampf
===============================================

Copyright 1995 Reuters Ltd.
  Germany still embarrassed by Hitler's bible of Nazi ideology
     By Tom Heneghan
     MUNICH, Germany, April 26 (Reuter) - Half a century after the Third
Reich collapsed, Germany is still embarrassed about the book regarded as its
bible -- Adolf Hitler's "Mein Kampf."
     Its publication is illegal in Germany and Bonn does its best to keep
other countries from reprinting the blueprint of Nazi ideology in German or
any other language.
     German authorities are locked in a court battle with a Stockholm
publisher who, in the name of freedom of speech, wants to print the
virulently anti-Semitic book in Swedish.
     They have turned down requests from Hungary and Norway for printing
rights and persuaded publishers in Mexico and Spain to stop producing it.
     They have thwarted an attempt by historians in Munich to issue an
annotated limited edition aimed at scholars.
     The zealous campaign against the book met its match earlier this year
when the University of Jerusalem came out with the first Hebrew-language
edition.
     "Israel is an exceptional case," an official at Bavaria's Finance
Ministry in Munich said as he tried to explain why Germany did not intervene.
     "They have to decide something like this for themselves," said the
official, who asked not to be named."
     "Mein Kampf" (My Struggle) is a turgid collection of Hitler's 
musings on
his youth, the Jews, Germany's World War One defeat, propaganda, the Nazi
party and the role of the Fuehrer.
     He dictated the first half to Rudolf Hess and other early Nazis 
while in
prison after his abortive Munich beer hall putsch in 1923 and it appeared in
print in 1925.
     He wanted to call the work "A Four-and-a-half Year Struggle against
Lies, Stupidity and Cowardice" but settled for the simpler title "A
Reckoning." The second half, "The National Socialist Movement," appeared in
1926.
     They were first published together as "Mein Kampf" in 1930, three years
before Hitler came to power. About 10 million copies had been printed by the
time he committed suicide in his Berlin bunker on April 30, 1945.
     Newly-married couples were given a copy after saying "Ja" at the
register office and second-hand bookshops were barred from selling it to
avoid the impression nobody wanted to read it.
     It is difficult to say how many Germans actually read it in full at the
time. After the war, few people wanted to.
     Postwar U.S. occupation authorities gave the Bavarian Finance Ministry
exclusive rights to "Mein Kampf" when it transferred Nazi property --
including the Franz Eher publishing house that printed it -- to West
Germany's new democratic government.
     Since then, the Bavarian ministry has effectively banned the further
reprinting of "Mein Kampf" by turning down requests to publish it and having
German embassies use diplomatic or legal means to suppress it abroad.
     "We must avoid the impression abroad that the Germans are making it
possible to publish this book and thus fostering the spread of Nazi
thinking," said state finance ministry spokesman Erwin Horak.
     "In view of the increasing activity of right-wing extremist groups in
Germany and abroad, these considerations still carry weight," he said. "The
possible disadvantages of printing the book outweigh the advantages."
     Serious historians are less sure.
     "No matter how justified the political considerations may seem, it 
is a
worrying situation when the state censors books in this way and robs scholars
of the possibility of using an important historical source," Eberhard
Jaeckel, a respected historian of the Third Reich, wrote as early as 1969.
     Two years ago, the Institute for Contemporary History in Munich, a
prominent centre for studies of the Nazi years, asked if it could publish an
annotated study version.
     "They withdrew the application after talking to us," said the finance
ministry official.
     "We had political concerns. The extremist right was on the rise and it
would have been the wrong signal at the time."
     This makes it difficult, but not impossible, to find out exactly what
the book contains.
     "Many university libraries have it and they have their own rules about
access," said Christian Hartmann, who is editing a series of Hitler's
collected speeches and articles for the Institute for Contemporary History.
     "If you come here, you can use it in our reading room."
     There are pirated editions printed abroad in German which circulate
among neo-Nazis at high prices, he said.
     Horak said scholars making serious studies of anti-Semitism or Nazi
thought were usually allowed to publish excerpts from "Mein Kampf" so 
long as
