Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.censorship,uk.net,bham.usenet.misc
Subject: Re: UK University bans newsgroups.
Date: 30 Apr 1995 16:41:23 GMT
Message-ID: <3o0ejj$8kb@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>

The head of the Academic Computing Service at the U. of Birmingham
(England) is quoted:

>>PS. Just a warning - yes, technically you can direct your newsreader
>>to another Usenet server outside the University, but you should be
>>aware that in doing so you may contravene the University Conditions
>>of Use for Computing Facilities, and JANET conditions of use when
>>you tranfer the information.

I think alt.sex has more serious value than most rec.* and soc.*
groups. But not only doesn't the University subscribe to alt.sex , it
forbids student and professors from bringing in an article from off
campus and reading it on an ACS terminal.

Does the University library ban all books about sex? Are students
forbidden from bring books about sex and drugs on campus?

The head of the Academic Computing Service is also quoted:

>>2. The following newsgroups are excluded at Birmingham on my authority:
>>
>>        alt.evil
>>        alt.psychoactives
>>        alt.drugs (and subgroups)
>>
>>   The reason is simple: we cannot and do not read all individual
>>   messages, thus we use the names of groups to identify potentially
>>   offensive material at the group level.

So, "potentially offensive" material isn't allowed on British campuses
and is banned without even seeing it? (I apologize for not having
access to any British statements of academic or intellectual freedom,
but I do have access to such statements from other places.

The Canadian Library Association's Statement on Intellectual Freedom
says:

     It is the responsibility of libraries to guarantee and facilitate access
     to all expressions of knowledge and intellectual activity,
     including those which some some elements of society may consider
     to be unconventional, unpopular or unacceptable.  To this end,
     libraries shall acquire and make available the widest variety of
     materials.
                    (ftp://ftp.eff.org/pub/CAF/library/int-freedom.can)

and

     Libraries should resist all efforts to limit the exercise of these
     responsibilities while recognizing the right of criticism by
     individuals and groups.

The U. of Birmingham is not only *not* resisting efforts to limit
access to unpopular expression, it is *actively* limiting access even
farther by banning access to extra newsgroup groups of its own.

Is this a University-wide policy applying to all media and forums on
campus? Or are computer media subject to a double standard at the
U. of Birmingham?

>>3. The University provides its computing facilities for teaching, learning
>>   and research purposes, and use of these facilities are subject to
>>   the University Conditions of Use which are designed to protect
>>   the University.  There is no a priori requirement of the
>>   University to provide Usenet, but it is provided to allow the
>>   real benefits to be available to assist in the mission of the
>>   University.

Isn't free expression and the freedom to read are central to the
"mission of the University"? The Joint Statement on Rights and
Freedoms of Students, the main U.S. statement of student academic
freedom, starts:

     Academic institutions exist for the transmission of knowledge, the
     pursuit of truth, the development of students, and the general
     well-being of society. Free inquiry and free expression are
     indispensable to the attainment of these goals. As members of the
     academic community, students should be encouraged to develop the
     capacity for critical judgment and to engage in a sustained and
     independent search for truth. Institutional procedures for achieving
       (ftp://ftp.eff.org/pub/CAF/academic/student.freedoms.aaup)

What would a student or professor have to do show to be allowed to
access a banned newsgroup? Would scholarly references or positive
reviews of alt.sex or alt.drugs be enough? If I was a student or
professor and found discussion of abortion offensive, could I complain
and get the group banned?

>>4. Censorship is not an issue since we are providing a facility and
>>have chosen to do this by making certain newsgroups available (in
>>fact, the vast majority of these are available).

Librarians distinguish between "selection" and "censorship". By the
standards with which I'm familiar, library or university selection
based on "offensive", is censorship.

The American Library Association's Library Bill of Rights, for
example, says:

   Materials should not be proscribed or removed because of partisan
   or doctrinal disapproval.
       (ftp://ftp.eff.org/pub/CAF/library/bill-of-rights.ala)

(And, of course, you go beyond not selecting material, you also ban
access of the material from other servers.)

Are British standards so different than those of Canada and U.S ? Is
it really considered right and proper for a University to ban from
campus property any material whose name suggests it might be
"potentially offensive"?

- Carl
