Subject: Limits of University control of homepages. A question.
Date: 30 Apr 95 23:19:01 CST
Message-ID: <1995Apr30.231901@cctr.umkc.edu>

I have a question about the limits of university control...

If there is a University Homepage it is obvious that the University has the
right to determine the contents of that homepage. If the homepage has pointers
that go to university departments there is an obvious interest in controlling
the content of the departmental homepages. No problem.

What if the departmental home pages point to student home pages, and some of
those student home pages point to nude pictures or other things that the
university administration deems offensive. The student home pages are not
official university documents -- can the university control the contents of the
home pages? Does a disclaimer have any effect? 

I know that many of the major home pages (like Yahoo) point to pages that, in
turn, point to pages that the university would consider to be offensive. Is
that just a matter of Stanford policy? Are there more general policy statements
on the issue?

Thanks for any info.

Harris Mirkin

